Story 1.2 (Revised): Implement Advanced AI-Driven CI/CD and Code Quality Automation
Status: Draft
Story
- As a developer,
- I want to implement a comprehensive, AI-driven CI/CD pipeline with advanced code quality and testing automation in the monorepo,
- so that code quality is rigorously enforced, development cycles are accelerated through AI-powered autofixing and test generation, and deployments are streamlined and secure.
Acceptance Criteria (ACs)
A. Git Hooks & Commit Workflow
1. lefthook is configured to manage Git hooks in the monorepo.
2. lint-staged is configured via lefthook to run linters on staged files.
3. Upon git commit, cz-git is triggered to guide the commit message creation.
4. The system (AI agent) automatically writes commit messages adhering to Conventional Commits standard with emoji format.
5. Commitlint is configured via lefthook to validate commit messages against standardization, failing the commit if rules are violated.
B. Linting & Formatting Automation
1. Linters (ESLint v9 for JS/TS, Pylint for Python, Biome.js for web projects) are integrated and configured for their respective codebases.
2. Linting and formatting checks are executed on staged files via lint-staged and lefthook.
3. For any failing linter runs, the problematic files and relevant line numbers are presented to the developer.
4. The system (AI agent) automatically fixes detectable linting/formatting issues in the codebase.
5. The AI agent automatically re-commits the fixed/updated code after autofixing linting/formatting issues.
C. Unit Testing & Coverage Automation
1. Unit tests are configured to run automatically after linting/formatting passes.
2. For any failing unit tests, the failures (file and relevant line numbers) are presented to the developer.
3. The system (AI agent) automatically fixes problematic code that caused test failures.
4. The AI agent automatically re-runs only the tests that failed until all passing.
5. The AI agent automatically re-commits the fixed/updated code after autofixing test failures.
6. Codecov is integrated to report and enforce test coverage thresholds.
7. Test coverage is initially set to a minimum of >= 60%, with a target to iterate towards >= 80%.
8. Insufficient test coverage (below threshold) triggers the system (AI agent) to automatically write the required tests to meet coverage.
9. The AI agent automatically runs the newly generated tests, autofixes any issues, and re-commits as per previous steps, until coverage is met and tests pass.
D. Containerization & Image Management
1. Dockerfiles for relevant microservices are present in their respective package directories.
2. Upon successful build/test/lint, updated Dockerfiles trigger a rebuild, tagging, and push of new Docker images to a designated Docker Hub repository.
E. CI/CD Orchestration & Deployment Flow
1. GitHub Actions workflows are implemented to manage the execution of all defined CI/CD steps (git hooks, linting, testing, containerization).
2. All applicable actions are configured within package.json scripts (globally or within packages/apps) and integrated into the Turborepo (turbo.json) task graph for efficient execution and caching.
3. Turborepo remote caching is implemented and configured for efficiency across CI runs.
4. Upon successful completion of the development CI/CD pipeline (build, test, lint, etc.), an automated push to a staging environment becomes available.
5. Production branch pushes are restricted, requiring a passing development CI/CD pipeline execution, a passing staging CI/CD pipeline execution, AND a CODEOWNERS signoff.
6. The Next.js web UI frontend automatically deploys to Vercel upon successful staging pipeline completion.
7. Backend APIs/Docker containers automatically deploy to Google Cloud (or AWS) upon successful staging pipeline completion.
8. Database instances (PostgreSQL) are managed via Supabase, Turso, or Neon, as appropriate for each microservice's needs.
